Chapter 15
Sustainable growth; book value against market value
النمو المستدام والقيمة الدفترية مقابل القيمة السوقية
Orontes grew its sales by 15.4 per cent in 2025. Whether that was a triumph or a warning depends on a figure the income statement does not carry: the rate at which the company can grow on the profit it keeps, without issuing a share or borrowing a dollar more in proportion. That rate is the sustainable growth rate, and a company that exceeds it for long must finance the difference from somewhere.
The second half of the chapter asks what a share of Orontes is worth. The balance sheet answers with book value, which is the equity the accountants recognise, divided by the shares. The market answers with a price. The two answers for Orontes differ by a factor of nearly four, and understanding why is the beginning of valuation — and the end of any belief that a balance sheet measures what a company is worth.
Figure F515-01 is your map.
Figure F515-01. Chapter 15 roadmap: one growth rate, one book value, and the gap to the market.
