WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 14 computed Orontes's return on equity as 16.15 per cent on average equity and 14.56 per cent on closing equity. Chapter 4 computed its sales growth as about 15 per cent a year. This chapter puts the two numbers in one sentence, and the sentence is the whole of the sustainable growth idea.
15.1  Sustainable growth: what the business can fund itself
Growth consumes money. A company selling a fifth more next year needs a fifth more inventory and a fifth more receivables, and sooner or later a fifth more plant. Chapter 12 priced that: Orontes carries about $4,544,000 of trade working capital, so a fifth more sales needs roughly a fifth more of it.
There are only four places the money can come from: profit the company keeps, new debt, new equity, or a change in the way the business works. The sustainable growth rate asks how fast the company can grow on the first of those alone, holding its financial policies constant — the same debt-to-equity ratio, the same payout, the same margins, the same asset turnover.
The answer is short. Sustainable growth equals the return on equity multiplied by the proportion of profit retained.
The retention ratio is one minus the payout. Orontes declared dividends of 50,000 + 400,000 = 450,000 in 2025 — the $50,000 paid in cash during the year and the $400,000 still payable at December 31 — so the payout ratio is 450,000 ÷ 2,969,100 = 15.16 per cent and the retention ratio is 100.0 − 15.16 = 84.84 per cent.
At the average-equity return on equity of 16.15 per cent, sustainable growth is 16.15 × 0.8484 = 13.70 per cent.
You can see the same thing happening in the statement of retained earnings, which is where the retained profit physically lands: 4,375,000 + 2,969,100 − 450,000 = 6,894,100, the retained earnings figure on the 2025 balance sheet. Equity grew by that retention, and if nothing about the business or its funding mix changed, the business could support about 13.7 per cent more of everything.
Figure F515-02 sets out the computation.
Figure F515-02. Orontes Foods, Inc. — sustainable growth, 2025
@table
Element | Figure | Source
Net income | 2,969,100 | Income statement
Dividends declared | 450,000 | Paid 50,000, payable 400,000
Payout ratio | 15.16% | Dividends over net income
Retention ratio | 84.84% | One less the payout
Return on equity | 16.15% | Chapter 14, average basis
Sustainable growth rate | 13.70% | Return on equity times retention
@end
EXAM TRAP   Which return on equity goes into the growth rate
The formula is sensitive to the equity basis, and the exam is explicit when it matters. On closing equity Orontes's return is 14.56 per cent, giving 14.56 × 0.8484 = 12.35 per cent. On beginning equity it is 2,969,100 ÷ 16,375,000 = 18.13 per cent, giving 18.13 × 0.8484 = 15.38 per cent.
The beginning-equity version has the cleanest logic — this year's profit on the equity the company started with — and the widest spread of answers. State your basis.
Dividends declared, not dividends paid, belong in the payout ratio. Orontes paid only $50,000 in cash and declared nine times that.
