D.	the dividend yield plus the payout.
SC15-2  Orontes's retention ratio for 2025 is:
A.	15.16 per cent
B.	84.84 per cent
C.	100.0 per cent
D.	13.70 per cent
SC15-3  The payout ratio uses:
A.	dividends paid in cash during the year.
B.	dividends declared for the year.
C.	dividends payable at the year end only.
D.	the dividend per share.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
15.2  Growing faster than that, and growing slower
Orontes's sales grew 42,000,000 − 36,400,000 = 5,600,000 in 2025, which is 5,600,000 ÷ 36,400,000 = 15.38 per cent, against a sustainable rate of 13.70 per cent. The company grew faster than its own profit could fund, and the statements show exactly where the difference came from: a $1,500,000 share issue, $1,400,000 of additional paid-in capital, and an $800,000 bank note. Nothing improper happened. But the growth was part-funded from outside, and that cannot be repeated indefinitely without either more issues or more debt.
A company growing faster than its sustainable rate has four choices, and the exam expects you to name them rather than simply observe the gap.
Raise the sustainable rate through the business. A better margin or a faster asset turnover raises the return on equity and therefore the growth the company can fund. This is the only route that costs nothing.
Raise it through policy. Cut the payout, which raises retention directly. Orontes retains 84.84 per cent already, so there is little room; a company paying out half its profit has a great deal.
Fund the gap. Issue equity, as Orontes did, or borrow, which raises the equity multiplier and with it the return on equity — and the risk Chapter 8 priced.
Grow more slowly. Unfashionable, and it is what happens anyway to a company that will not do the other three: the overdraft arrives first and the decision is taken for it.
That last case is Barada's, and you have seen it twice already. Barada's sales grew 11 per cent while it retained everything it earned on a return on equity of 9.15 per cent. Eleven against nine, year after year, is the whole explanation of the inventory up 41 per cent, the receivables up 38 per cent, and the overdraft of $240,000 where there was none.
Figure F515-03 compares the two companies.
Figure F515-03. Growth against sustainable growth
@table
Company | Actual sales growth | Sustainable rate | Gap | How it was funded
Orontes, 2025 | 15.38% | 13.70% | 1.7 points | Share issue and a bank note
Barada, 2025 | 11.0% | 9.15% | 1.9 points | A bank overdraft
A mature utility | 2.0% | 6.0% | (4.0) points | Surplus returned as dividends
@end
The third row is the opposite problem and it is examinable too. A company growing more slowly than it can fund accumulates cash it has no use for, which lowers its return on assets — the point section 14.1 made about issuing shares and holding the proceeds. The remedy is a larger dividend, a buyback, or an acquisition, and Book 3 takes that decision properly.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: how fast can Orontes grow without issuing shares?
Step 1. The engine. Return on equity on the beginning equity of $16,375,000 is 2,969,100 ÷ 16,375,000 = 18.13 per cent, and the retention ratio is 84.84 per cent.
Step 2. The rate. 18.13 × 0.8484 = 15.38 per cent, which happens to be almost exactly the sales growth achieved.
Step 3. What that means. On the beginning-equity basis Orontes grew at its sustainable rate, and on the average-equity basis it grew faster than it. The two bases disagree precisely because the share issue landed in the middle of the year — the average basis counts half of the new money as if it had been there all along, and the beginning basis counts none of it.
Check. This is not a trick of arithmetic; it is the reason the exam names the basis. For a company that has not issued or repurchased shares, the three answers are close together and the choice does not matter. For Orontes in 2025 the spread is 12.35 to 15.38 per cent, and an answer that quotes one figure without naming its basis cannot be checked by the marker or by anybody else.
