15.3  Book value per share
Book value per share is total stockholders' equity divided by the shares outstanding at the same date. It is what the accountants say the shareholders own, per share, after every liability.
Orontes's equity at December 31, 2025 is $20,392,975 and 8,100,000 shares are issued and outstanding, so book value per share is 20,392,975 ÷ 8,100,000 = 2.5177. A year earlier it was 16,375,000 ÷ 8,000,000 = 2.0469.
Three points of mechanics before the interpretation.
Use shares outstanding at the balance sheet date, not the weighted average. The weighted average belongs to earnings per share, which is a flow over a period; book value is a balance at a date, and so is the share count that goes with it.
Treasury shares are not outstanding. They reduce equity and they reduce the share count, and because they are normally repurchased above book value they usually raise book value per share — a point that trips candidates who expect a buyback to lower it.
Where preferred stock exists, deduct it. Book value per common share is equity less the preferred claim, over the common shares.
Figure F515-04 sets the two years out.
Figure F515-04. Orontes: book value per share
@table
Element | 2025 | 2024
Total stockholders' equity | 20,392,975 | 16,375,000
Shares issued and outstanding | 8,100,000 | 8,000,000
Book value per share | 2.5177 | 2.0469
@end
Book value per share rose by about 23 per cent in a year, from retained profit and from the share issue, which was priced well above book. For a bank or an insurer, which hold mostly financial assets carried at or near fair value, that figure would be close to a real measure of worth. For a food manufacturer it is not, and the next section says why.
EXAM TRAP   Which share count, which equity
Shares issued is not always shares outstanding: the difference is treasury stock. Orontes has none, so for Orontes the two are the same and the exam may still ask.
Book value per share uses the closing equity. Averaging it is an error — there is no period for a balance to be averaged over.
Earnings per share and book value per share use different share counts on purpose. Chapter 16 computes the first.
