15.4  What book value per share leaves out
Orontes's shares change hands at $9.25. Its book value per share is $2.5177. The market says a share is worth nearly four times what the balance sheet says, and the reason is not that one of them is wrong. It is that they measure different things, and the gap is the sum of everything the balance sheet is not designed to carry.
Historical cost. Property is carried at what it cost less accumulated depreciation. Orontes's plant cost $26,790,000 and stands at $14,802,000; land bought thirty years ago stands at its 1990s price. Under IFRS a company may revalue; under US GAAP it may not.
Intangibles the company built. Orontes's brand is on the balance sheet at $260,000 because it was acquired. The recipes, the customer relationships, the trained workforce and the reputation that make the brand worth anything were built internally, and internally generated intangibles are not recognised at all. A software company's whole value sits in this gap, which is why its market-to-book ratio runs in double figures.
Goodwill, which is only ever downhill. Acquired goodwill is recognised and then tested for impairment. It can fall and can never rise. Orontes's goodwill went from $950,000 to $300,000 in 2025 through exactly that mechanism.
What the future holds. The market price discounts expected future cash flows. The balance sheet records past transactions. A company whose profits are expected to double trades far above book; a company expected to shrink trades below it, and a market-to-book ratio under 1.0 is the market saying the assets are worth less in this company's hands than their carrying amount.
Figure F515-05 collects the four.
Figure F515-05. Why book value per share understates a going concern
@table
What is missing | Why | Orontes's evidence
Current values | Assets at cost less depreciation | Plant at 14,802,000 against a cost of 26,790,000
Internally built intangibles | Not recognised under GAAP | Brand at 260,000; recipes and reputation at nil
Rising goodwill | Only tested downwards | Goodwill fell from 950,000 to 300,000
Expected future profit | The balance sheet is a record, not a forecast | A share price of 9.25 against a book value of 2.5177
@end
