SC15-11  A market-to-book ratio below 1.0 suggests the market believes:
A.	the assets are worth less in this company's hands than their carrying amount.
B.	the company is highly profitable.
C.	book value is overstated by intangibles.
D.	the shares are about to be repurchased.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
15.5  Market value, and the profit the accountants do not charge for
Market value per share is the price. Market capitalisation is the price times the shares: 8,100,000 × 9.25 = 74,925,000. Against a book equity of $20,392,975, the market adds 74,925,000 − 20,392,975 = 54,532,025 of value that no statement records.
The ratio of the two is the market-to-book ratio, which Chapter 16 treats as a market multiple: 9.25 ÷ 2.5177 = 3.67 for 2025, and 7.80 ÷ 2.0469 = 3.81 for 2024. The multiple fell slightly while the price rose, because book value rose faster.
Now the second distinction this outcome requires, and the one candidates find least familiar. Accounting profit is revenue less the costs the standards require to be recognised. Economic profit is revenue less all costs, including the cost of the equity capital employed — which the standards never charge, because equity has no contractual cost and so no expense is recorded.
That omission is large. Orontes's lenders are charged for in the income statement, at $390,000 of interest. Its shareholders, who put up $18,383,988 on average, are charged nothing at all, although they could have invested the money elsewhere.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: Orontes's economic profit
Step 1. Accounting profit. Net income for 2025 is $2,969,100, struck after interest and after tax, and it is the profit every ratio in Book 1 has used.
Step 2. The charge the statements omit. Suppose a shareholder in a food manufacturer of this risk requires 10 per cent. On average equity the charge is 18,383,988 × 0.10 = 1,838,399.
Step 3. Economic profit. 2,969,100 − 1,838,399 = 1,130,701. Orontes earned about 1.1 million dollars more than the minimum its owners require.
Check. The sign is what matters. A positive economic profit means the company is creating value and the market-to-book ratio above 1.0 is justified; a negative one means the company earns an accounting profit while destroying value, which is precisely the case a market-to-book ratio below 1.0 describes. Note also the consistency test: a return on equity of 16.15 per cent against a required 10 per cent is the same statement as a positive economic profit, expressed as a rate instead of an amount.
