Chapter summary
Figure F515-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F515-07. Chapter 15 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Sustainable growth is the return on equity times the retention ratio: Orontes retains 84.84 per cent of its profit and earns 16.15 per cent on average equity, so it can fund 13.70 per cent growth a year, and it grew 15.38 per cent — the difference arriving as a $1,500,000 share issue and an $800,000 note. A company above its sustainable rate must improve the business, cut the payout, fund the gap or slow down, and Barada, growing 11 per cent on a sustainable 9.15, took the fourth option by default when the overdraft appeared. Book value per share is closing equity over shares outstanding, $2.5177 for Orontes against $2.0469 a year earlier, and it uses the closing share count because it is a balance and not a flow. It leaves out current values, internally built intangibles, any rise in goodwill and the whole of the future, which is why the market pays $9.25 — a market-to-book ratio of 3.67 and 54,532,025 of value that appears in no statement. And the accountants charge for debt but never for equity, so accounting profit of $2,969,100 becomes an economic profit of 1,130,701 once the owners' 10 per cent is charged: positive, which is the same statement as a return on equity above the required return.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
