A.	retained profit alone.
B.	a share issue and a bank note.
C.	a dividend cut.
D.	sale of the brand.
Case-style practice: the dividend question
Orontes's board is asked to raise the dividend. One director proposes paying out half of net income rather than the 15.16 per cent paid for 2025. The company plans sales growth of about 14 per cent a year for the next three years.
Required. Compute the sustainable growth rate under the proposed payout, using the average-equity return on equity of 16.15 per cent. Say whether the plan and the proposal are consistent, and what the company would have to do if the board adopted both. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC15-1  Answer A. Retained profit is the only internal source of equity, so the growth it funds is the return times the share retained.
B is wrong. It is the return on equity, and the retention, not the payout.
C is wrong. That is a comparison, not the formula.
D is wrong. The dividend yield is a market measure and belongs to Chapter 16.
SC15-2  Answer B. 100.0 − 15.16 = 84.84 per cent.
A is wrong. That is the payout ratio.
C is wrong. Orontes declared dividends, so retention is below 100.
D is wrong. 13.70 per cent is the sustainable growth rate.
SC15-3  Answer B. Dividends declared for the year is the charge against the year's profit.
A is wrong. Orontes paid only $50,000 in cash and declared $450,000.
C is wrong. The payable is one part of the declaration.
D is wrong. The dividend per share has to be multiplied by the shares first.
SC15-4  Answer B. Growth above the sustainable rate must be financed from outside or the business must change.
A is wrong. Fast growth is not failure; it is a funding problem.
C is wrong. Cutting sales is one option among four, not a necessity.
D is wrong. A positive return on equity is what produces the rate at all.
SC15-5  Answer B. With no dividends the retention ratio is 1.00, so the rate equals the return on equity.
A is wrong. Zero would require a zero return or a full payout.
C is wrong. 11.0 per cent is the actual growth, which is the problem.
D is wrong. 1.85 points is the gap between the two.
SC15-6  Answer A. Cash accumulates and the denominator of the return on assets grows.
B is wrong. Running out of cash is the fast-growth case.
C is wrong. Slow growth relieves covenant pressure.
D is wrong. The multiplier falls as equity builds.
SC15-7  Answer A. Book value is a balance at a date, so the share count is at that date.
B is wrong. The weighted average belongs to earnings per share.
C is wrong. Authorised shares may never have been issued.
D is wrong. Diluted shares belong to diluted earnings per share.
SC15-8  Answer A. 20,392,975 ÷ 8,100,000 = 2.5177.
B is wrong. That is the 2024 figure.
C is wrong. 0.3666 is earnings per share.
D is wrong. 9.25 is the market price.
SC15-9  Answer A. Paying more than book for each share removes more cash than book value per share, so what is left per remaining share rises.
B is wrong. It would fall only on a repurchase below book value.
C is wrong. Both equity and the share count change, and not in proportion.
D is wrong. A buyback of this size does not make equity negative.
SC15-10  Answer B. Internally generated intangibles are not recognised.
A is wrong. They are often the most valuable thing the company has.
C is wrong. Goodwill arises only on acquisition.
D is wrong. They are not contingencies.
SC15-11  Answer A. The market is valuing the net assets below their carrying amount.
B is wrong. High profitability produces a ratio above 1.0.
C is wrong. Unrecognised intangibles understate book value, not overstate it.
D is wrong. A buyback is a possible response, not the meaning of the ratio.
SC15-12  Answer A. Economic profit charges for equity, which the standards never do.
B is wrong. Economic profit charges for debt as well.
C is wrong. It is normally computed after tax.
D is wrong. Sales are measured the same way.
SC15-13  Answer A. 8,100,000 × 9.25 = 74,925,000.
B is wrong. That is book equity.
C is wrong. That is the difference between the two.
D is wrong. That is total assets.
SC15-14  Answer B. A positive accounting profit below the required return is a negative economic profit.
A is wrong. Value is being destroyed, not created.
C is wrong. Insolvency is about liabilities, not returns.
D is wrong. Nothing is misstated; the standards simply do not charge for equity.
