Case-style set: the dividend question
At a payout of 50 per cent the retention ratio becomes 50 per cent, and the sustainable growth rate becomes 16.15 × 0.50 = 8.08 per cent.
The plan and the proposal are not consistent. Sales growth of 14 per cent a year against a sustainable 8.08 per cent leaves about six points of growth a year to be funded from outside — on 2025 sales, roughly $2,500,000 a year of additional working capital and plant that the retained profit will no longer cover.
If the board adopted both, it would have to do one of three things. It could borrow, which raises the equity multiplier and the return on equity but takes interest coverage down from 11.15 times and brings the covenant limits of Chapter 9 closer. It could issue shares again, as it did in 2025, which dilutes the existing holders and is not available cheaply in every year. Or it could improve the business: two points of recovered gross margin would raise the return on equity by roughly three points and carry about half of the gap by itself.
My recommendation would be to raise the dividend by less. A payout of 30 per cent gives a sustainable rate of 16.15 × 0.70 = 11.31 per cent, which with a modest amount of new debt supports the plan without either a share issue or a covenant problem. A board that wants both a 50 per cent payout and 14 per cent growth is asking the shareholders to fund the dividend with their own new money.
